## The new era: CSS in 2025

So far you’ve written CSS the way it was written in 2005 and 2014. But the language hasn’t stood still. In the last few years browsers have learned to do what once needed preprocessors (Sass, Less) and scripts. This lesson is about how people write CSS today. It belongs to our 2025 era: the page is the same, but the styles are rewritten.

We’re interested in four new things.

## Variables

**Custom properties**, also called “CSS variables”, are declared with two hyphens and read through `var()`:

```css
:root {
  --color-text: #1d2430;
  --color-accent: #1a4d8f;
  --space: 1rem;
}

.button {
  color: white;
  background-color: var(--color-accent);
  padding: var(--space);
}
```

`:root` is the `html` element itself, so variables declared there are visible across the whole page. The advantages:

- **the value lives in one place.** Want a different accent color? Change one line, not thirty;
- **they’re live.** Unlike a preprocessor’s variables, CSS variables stay in the browser. You can redefine them on any block (a card inside a dark block gets different colors) and change them from JavaScript;
- **they inherit** down the tree, like the text color does.

A good rule is to name variables by **role**, not by value: `--color-accent`, not `--blue`. Then in a year the accent can turn green and the name will still be true. Named values like these are called **design tokens**, and we’ll take them apart later, in the part on design systems.

A **dark theme** with variables is almost free: every color goes through a variable, and `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` changes only their values. The rules for cards and buttons don’t need touching at all. In the `head` you also write `<meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark">`, which tells the browser the site has two themes, so that it doesn’t flash white while loading.

Move the sliders in the diagram: one card, the same rules, and the look changes because of the values. Below it there’s more about layers.

<ThemeSwitch />

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: why is --color-accent a better name than --blue?</summary>

Because a role lasts longer than a value: tomorrow the accent may turn green, and the name will still be true.

</details>

## Nesting

Before, to write the rules for a card and its insides you had to repeat the selector: `.card { … }`, `.card h3 { … }`, `.card:hover { … }`. Now you nest the rules inside each other:

```css
.card {
  padding: var(--space);

  & h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  &:hover {
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
  }
}
```

The `&` means “the parent selector”. Nesting makes the file shorter and gathers everything about the card in one place. Don’t nest deeper than two or three levels: long chains hurt readability and specificity.

## :has(): a parent by its child

The `:has()` selector selects an element **by what lies inside it**. CSS had no parent selector at all, and this trick used to seem impossible:

```css
section:has(.cards) { padding-block: 1.5rem; }          /* a section that has cards in it */
form:has(input:invalid) button { opacity: 0.7; }        /* the button in a form that has an invalid field */
label:has(input:checked) { font-weight: 600; }           /* the label of a ticked checkbox */
```

`:has()` has worked in all browsers since the end of 2023. It’s one of the most useful new features: much of what used to be done with a script is now done by CSS.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what will form:has(input:invalid) select?</summary>

A form with at least one invalid field inside it. You can use it to style the button, for example.

</details>

## Layers

The more CSS there is, the more often rules fight: remember the cascade from the first lesson. The fight ended with `!important` and ever longer selectors. **Layers** (`@layer`) set the order in advance:

```css
@layer reset, base, components, utilities;
```

One line at the top of the file declares the layers from weakest to strongest. Then each rule goes into its own layer:

```css
@layer base {
  a { color: var(--color-accent); }
}

@layer components {
  .button { color: var(--color-on-accent); }
}
```

The rule is simple. **A later layer beats an earlier one, whatever the specificity.** Specificity decides only inside one layer. And rules **outside any layer beat every layer**: so keep unlayered styles for exceptions, and put the bulk of your CSS into layers. You can try this by hand at the bottom of the diagram above.

This saves you from the “specificity war”: a `#cta` selector in the `base` layer can’t beat a plain `.button` from the `utilities` layer. Layers reached browsers in 2022.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: which is stronger, a rule outside the layers or a rule from the last layer?</summary>

The rule outside the layers. It beats every layer, even the latest.

</details>

## Practice

You’re starting the 2025 era. We’ve given you the page and the styles: it’s the result you finished in the last lesson (if you saved your own styles, you can paste them in). Your job is to turn the styles into modern ones: variables on `:root`, a dark theme, layers, nesting and `:has()`.

The checks here can see the variables, the `color-scheme` tag and the `:has()` rule. They can’t see layers, nesting or the dark theme, because the browser hands them only computed values. Check those three by eye: open the developer tools and find your layers in the “Styles” pane, and turn on the dark theme in the “Rendering” panel (the option “Emulate CSS media feature prefers-color-scheme”).

## What was wrong with 2014

Back then it was almost impossible to write a big stylesheet without Sass or Less: you needed variables, nesting and mixins. A preprocessor turned your file into plain CSS on the server, and the browser saw only the finished result. Today the browser can do half of that itself. The other half of the problem was specificity and `!important`, until layers replaced them.
